---
// Lesson 10.1d — 2-Button Jam: Playtest
// rpg jam day: full playtest rotation with the beat table.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1d — 2-Button Jam: Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Freeze the build; test the humans. Today is data
    (<a href="9.5a.html">9.5a</a>'s methods on your own game) —
    deaths per minute, where testers stall, what they say — and the
    one-fix discipline that turns two hours of testing into two
    hours of improvement.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Rotation (three rounds):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Round 1 — strangers play (15 min).</strong>
            Swap stations with another team. Silent watching
            (<a href="2.4a.html">2.4a</a>). Record on the beat
            table: stall point, death point, the exact QUOTE
            ("wait, what does that do?"), and the moment of
            first smile. That smile is your game working.</li>
        <li><strong>Round 2 — the one fix (15 min).</strong> Each
            team picks ONE thing from Round 1 data. Apply it.
            Ship the fix, commit: <code>"10.1d: playtest fix #1"</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Round 3 — verify (10 min).</strong> New tester
            (or the same one). Did THAT moment change? If yes:
            pick fix #2. If no: the fix failed — revert it and
            write why. One-variable testing
            (<a href="3.4a.html">3.4a</a> tuning, jam-scale).</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>What to watch for in a two-input game specifically:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Input confusion:</strong> the tester presses
            the WRONG verb under pressure. That is not tester
            error — your two verbs are too similar (fix with
            feel, <a href="6.3a.html">6.3a</a>: sharper timing or
            clearer consequence)</li>
        <li><strong>Button mashing:</strong> both verbs at once,
            always. Your game has one verb wearing two
            costumes</li>
        <li><strong>The stall:</strong> tester stops playing and
            asks a question out loud — write the QUESTION down;
            it is your tutorial, spoken by the void</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Playtest traps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Round 2's fix broke Round 1's working moment.
                Why is revert-on-failure the correct move — and
                why do jam teams resist it (sunk cost — name
                it)?</li>
            <li>A tester loved the thing you were going to cut.
                What does the data say when one voice and one
                number disagree (<a href="9.6a.html">9.6a</a>
                scope: n=1 is not a pattern — but quotes still
                teach)?</li>
            <li>You caught yourself explaining the game to the
                tester. How much of your explanation is a design
                doc that SHOULD have been on screen?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Data + polish list</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Enter your beat table into your repo as
                playtest.csv (columns like
                <a href="../data/playtests.csv">the class
                dataset</a>). One row per tester-run.</li>
            <li>Finalize the 10.1e wishlist: three 10-minute
                polishes only. Which one makes the SMILE happen
                sooner?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Run three silent playtest rounds and record quotes + numbers</li>
            <li>Apply one fix, verify it, revert it if it failed</li>
            <li>Diagnose verb-confusion vs mashing vs stalls and prescribe fixes</li>
            <li>Ship a playtest.csv into your repo</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../data/playtests.csv">Playtest dataset (CSV)</a> — the format you mimic</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — the playtest ritual</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1e.html">10.1e — 2-Button Jam: Polish</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.1d" title="10.1d 2-Button Jam: Playtest — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
